      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To simplify \\((1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)\\), we can use a known trigonometric identity involving the tangent function. The identity we will use is:\n\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan x)(1 + \\tan 45^\\circ) = 2\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's verify this identity. We know that \\(\\tan 45^\\circ = 1\\), so the identity becomes:\n\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan x)(1 + 1) = 2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan x) \\cdot 2 = 2\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by 2, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n1 + \\tan x = 1 \\implies \\tan x = 0\n\\]\n\nHowever, the identity is not directly applicable to this specific problem, so we need to find a different approach. Instead, we can use the tangent addition formula in a different form. Consider the product:\n\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan x)(1 + \\tan y) = 1 + \\tan x + \\tan y + \\tan x \\tan y\n\\]\n\nWe need to find a way to simplify this expression without using the tangent addition formula directly. Let's use the fact that:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ = \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe can use the identity:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ = \\tan(20^\\circ + 25^\\circ) \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ = \\tan 45^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ} = 1 \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ} = \\frac{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nHowever, a simpler approach is to use the known value of the product of tangents in specific angles. We know from trigonometric tables or known results that:\n\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the simplified form of the expression is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
