      "text": "To simplify \\((1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)\\), we start by using the tangent addition formula and some trigonometric identities.\n\nFirst, recall the tangent addition formula:\n\\[\n\\tan(A + B) = \\frac{\\tan A + \\tan B}{1 - \\tan A \\tan B}\n\\]\nWe can set \\(A = 20^\\circ\\) and \\(B = 25^\\circ\\). Then:\n\\[\n\\tan(20^\\circ + 25^\\circ) = \\tan 45^\\circ = 1\n\\]\nAccording to the tangent addition formula:\n\\[\n\\tan(45^\\circ) = \\frac{\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ} = 1\n\\]\nThis implies:\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ = 1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\nRearranging the terms, we get:\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = 1\n\\]\nNow, consider the expression \\((1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)\\). Expanding this, we have:\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) = 1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\nUsing the identity \\(\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = 1\\), we substitute:\n\\[\n1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = 1 + 1 = 2\n\\]\nTherefore, the simplified value of \\((1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
