      "text": "To determine the simplified form of \\((1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)\\), we will use a trigonometric identity and properties of angles that are related. First, note that \\(20^\\circ\\) and \\(25^\\circ\\) are complementary in the sense that \\(25^\\circ = 65^\\circ - 40^\\circ\\) and \\(20^\\circ = 65^\\circ - 45^\\circ\\). However, a more direct approach involves using the tangent addition formula.\n\nWe use the tangent addition formula:\n\\[\n\\tan(A + B) = \\frac{\\tan A + \\tan B}{1 - \\tan A \\tan B}\n\\]\nIf we let \\(A = 65^\\circ\\) and \\(B = 35^\\circ\\) (so \\(A + B = 100^\\circ\\)), then:\n\\[\n\\tan(65^\\circ + 35^\\circ) = \\tan 100^\\circ = \\frac{\\tan 65^\\circ + \\tan 35^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 65^\\circ \\tan 35^\\circ} = -\\tan 10^\\circ\n\\]\nThis implies:\n\\[\n\\tan 100^\\circ = -\\tan 10^\\circ\n\\]\nNow, let's consider the product \\((1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)\\). We use the tangent addition formula with \\(A = 20^\\circ\\) and \\(B = 25^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\tan(20^\\circ + 25^\\circ) = \\tan 45^\\circ = 1\n\\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}\n\\]\nRearranging this equation, we get:\n\\[\n1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n1 = \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\nNow, we multiply both sides by \\(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n1 + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = (\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ)\n\\]\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\\[\n1 + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ + (\\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ)^2\n\\]\nSimplifying, we have:\n\\[\n1 + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ + (\\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ)^2\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + (\\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ)^2\n\\]\nNotice that we can use a known identity for the product of tangents of complementary angles:\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = \\tan (65^\\circ - 45^\\circ) = \\frac{\\tan 65^\\circ - \\tan 45^\\circ}{1 + \\tan 65^\\circ \\tan 45^\\circ} = \\frac{\\tan 65^\\circ - 1}{1 + \\tan 65^\\circ}\n\\]\nHowever, a simpler approach is to recognize that:\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) = 1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = 1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\nGiven the identity and properties, we find:\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) = 2\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
